<br/><br/>


<B>Frequently Asked Questions</B><BR>
If you have a question that is not covered here, please <A HREF="contact.aspx">contact Arista</A>.

<P><B>General</B><BR><BR><UL>
<LI><B>Does using TimerMod violate the EverQuest EULA?</B><BR>
No, TimerMod is absolutely legal to use. The EverQuest UI is designed to support player customization. TimerMod is a standard EQ interface modification, which is completely allowed and supported by the game. For more information on EQ interface modifications, please see <A HREF="http://help.station.sony.com/cgi-bin/soe.cfg/php/enduser/std_adp.php?p_faqid=1832" TARGET=_blank>How do I Modify the User Interface?</A> in the official EverQuest knowledge base.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>Is TimerMod safe to download?</B><BR>
Yes. TimerMod does not require you to download or run any executable files, because all the custom processing is done server-side. The generated ZIP file package contains only plain-text HTML and XML files, and a TGA image file. If you wish, you can examine the contents of these files for yourself to verify that they are harmless.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>Will TimerMod work with other UI mods?</B><BR>
Yes, you can use the timers with any other UI modifications. In general, this can be done by simply placing the TimerMod files in the same folder. In the unlikely situation where TimerMod replaces a modified file containing elements referenced by other files in the mod, you will have to move those elements to another file or just select another window for the timers.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>Can I include TimerMod in a mod that I'm distributing to others?</B><BR>
You can include TimerMod in your distribution as long as appropriate credit is given in your description/readme file and you provide a link for users to reconfigure the timers for themselves. Please visit the <A HREF="develop.aspx">developer section</A> for guidelines and a web-based tool that helps you create a link with the TimerMod settings you want to enforce.<BR><BR></LI>
</UL></P>

<P><B>Installation</B><BR><BR><UL>
<LI><B>How do I install the timers?</B><BR>
Please follow the step-by-step instructions provided in the INSTRUCTIONS file included with your timer file package. You don't need to log out of the game to install your timers. For more information on installing UI mods in general (not timer-specific), visit the <A HREF="http://www.eqinterface.com/forums/faq.php?faq=install" TARGET=_blank>EQInterface FAQ</A>.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>Can I use multiple timer windows at the same time?</B><BR>
Yes, multiple timer windows can coexist without any conflicts. Just choose a different window for each configuration and place all the files in the same mod folder.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>How do I use the same window but different timers for different characters?</B><BR>
Each of your characters can have a different mod folder loaded, so just configure and download separate timer file packages for each character, giving each configuration its own mod folder. Then, log into each character and have that character load the corresponding mod folder. This allows each character to use the same window for the timers but have different timers in that window.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>Can I use custom icons for my timers?</B><BR>
Yes, you can use custom spell/item icons with your timers by placing the custom image files in the same mod folder as the timer files. If no custom files are found in that folder then the timers will use the default spell/item icons.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>How do I uninstall the timers?</B><BR>
To uninstall the timers, just remove the files that came with your timer file package from your mod folder. To switch to the default UI or another mod without removing any files, simply type <B>/load</B> to select and load another folder.<BR><BR></LI>
</UL></P>

<P><B>Setup</B><BR><BR><UL>
<LI><B>How do I open and close the timer window?</B><BR>
Each window has different methods for opening/closing, so this depends on the window you selected for your timers. The INSTRUCTIONS file included in your file package lists all the available methods for opening and closing the window used by your particular configuration.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>I can't open the timer window. What is wrong?</B><BR>
Make sure you've selected a window that is normally available to you. Some windows require you to have a particular expansion pack installed or a particular setting enabled. To see if a window is available to you, load the default UI and try opening the window. If you can't open the window in the default UI then you can't use that window for timers.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>How do I move the timer window?</B><BR>
The steps for moving the window are listed in the INSTRUCTIONS file in your file package. The important thing to remember is to make sure the window is not locked. Also, you must actually click on the outside edge of the timers in order to see the move cursor. The move cursor won't appear when you just hover your mouse because the timer window is borderless.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>How do I temporarily switch back to the default window?</B><BR>
If your timer occupies a window that you only need to use once in a while, you can reload the default window when needed. Just switch back to the default UI by typing <B>/load default</B>. When you are done using the window, you can switch back to the timers by typing <B>/load <I>FOLDER</I></B> (where FOLDER is the name of your mod folder).<BR><BR></LI>
</UL></P>

<P><B>Using the Timers</B><BR><BR><UL>
<LI><B>How can I find out a timer's name and duration?</B><BR>
To see a timer's name and duration, hover your cursor over the timer's icon. A tooltip will appear with the information.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>How do I start timing an event?</B><BR>
To begin timing, simply set the marker to the current position of the dot. The dot will continue moving and reach the marker's position again when exactly one duration of the timer has elapsed. As the dot approaches the marker, you will know that time's almost up.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>For short-duration events, I don't have time to set the marker.</B><BR>
Setting the marker is strictly optional. If you are timing an event with a very short duration, just glance at the timer and make a mental note of the dot's position.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>How do I time spawn cycles? Each area has a different spawn time.</B><BR>
To time variable-duration events, you can use a general-purpose timer where each section of the bar represents a nice round unit of time. Use the tick marks on the bar to help you set the marker.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI>(more Q&amp;A to be added soon)<BR><BR></LI>
</UL></P>

<P><B>Enhancement Requests</B><BR><BR><UL>
<LI><B>Why can't there be start/stop/reset buttons?</B><BR>
Due to the limitations placed on player-made UI mods, is not possible to add new buttons with functionality to control the timer animation. UI mods can only modify the appearance of the UI, not add new functionality. Therefore, the timer is not a "program", it is simply a collection of animated images.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>Can the timer automatically show system time, or game time?</B><BR>
Since the timer is simply a collection of animated images, it does not have any connection to RL time or EQ time. It is only capable of cycling through its frames continuously.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI><B>What are the limitations of animated images in the UI?</B><BR>
Once the EverQuest UI loads up, all animations begin cycling through their frames - there is no way to pause an animation or force it to start from a particular frame at a particular time. Reloading the entire UI via /loadskin will reset an animation, but it also resets all the animations at the same time, so there is no way to reset each animation individually. For more detailed information, please read the article at the bottom of the <A HREF="http://www.horizonsoutpost.com/eq/faq.asp" TARGET=_blank>FAQ on the old site</A>.<BR><BR></LI>
<LI>(more Q&amp;A to be added soon)<BR><BR></LI>
</UL></P>



